92186110591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111490 #edb/ref 1061 14242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcc20bde-4fea-571c-96b0-f7260ad49af3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111878 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(4)-{log}_{3}⁡(6) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111878 #edb/ref 1065 2784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111878 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3ed13d12-2b3d-5700-8a30-aa21719ebea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111878 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1071 "$6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f70da637-39bf-585b-b7da-8cee169442e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234207 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4) & =6({log}_{5}⁡(100)-{log}_{5}⁡(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{100}{4}) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}⁡(25)={log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}⁡(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542646 #edb/ref 1080 "{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3}+x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542646 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542646 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "027931f1-c81a-5437-9a6a-eaecb93a762a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542651 #edb/ref 1080 "k-m" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542651 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542651 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542674 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{e}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f39b09f-5b91-5a99-a4f6-25704e2a1be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542701 #edb/ref 1080 "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7}{5})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542701 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542701 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18804b67-878e-56f2-a908-9dd72d5c6203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542708 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542708 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542708 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a2284e9-4930-533e-bba1-f292b232ff69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542721 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542721 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542721 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1eec112c-25aa-553b-a422-22de0d6e6956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542727 #edb/ref 1080 "{log}_{3}⁡(y)-{log}_{3}⁡(x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542