ef 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "692975c7-d71f-5bff-afaa-3fff34d21e62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543514 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17) & =6(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{68}{17}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)=p$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=q$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99f3a3dc-a29b-56b3-b6b2-eb00c09e4e97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543532 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for common logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for common logarithms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(y) \\\\  & =p-q\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=k$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})=m$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada9c1dd-522d-5138-9cfe-44ee7af19872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543547 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{(\\frac{y}{x})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =k-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
